From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 15:07:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Drive the TT's SCSI from a Verilated NCR 5380

Replace both halves of Hatari's SCSI at once: the eight registers come from
a Verilated wish5380 loaded at run time, and so does the disk, which is a
SCSI target of its own with an SD card behind it.  Hatari's own model and its
command-level disk are left out of the path entirely - a co-simulation that
kept the emulator's disk would only be testing the emulator's disk.

Enabled by setting WISH5380_LIB to the shared library and WISH5380_SD to the
card image.  With either unset nothing changes and Hatari's own model runs,
which is what the existing --scsi tests still exercise.

The seam is two functions, because the TT's is: the machine reaches the chip
through eight byte-wide registers at 0xff8780, two apart and on the odd byte,
and that is the whole of what it can do to it.

What needed writing rather than forwarding is the DMA controller, since the
TT has one and the 5380 does not.  Hatari's own loop asks the emulated device
how many bytes it holds and moves exactly that many; nothing can do that here,
because the target is inside the Verilated core and answers through REQ like
a real one.  So the loop ends the way the hardware's does - when the count
runs out, or when the target leaves the phase the chip was set up for, which
PHASE MATCH reports and the chip's interrupt does not.  An interrupt is a
latch and may be left over from the command before.

It also packs bytes into longwords the way the TT's DMA chip does, leaving
whatever does not fill one in the residue register at 0xff8710.  That is not
decoration: EmuTOS's cleanup_tt_dma() reads the low two bits of the final
pointer to find out how many bytes are still in there and copies them to
memory itself, so a controller that had written them already and left the
residue empty would give it a short read it could not see.

No timer is needed to keep the core moving, which the Sun-3 co-simulation
does need.  EmuTOS polls the chip's own registers while it waits for
selection and for REQ, so a little core time bought with each register access
carries it forward; and for the data phases the pump has already run the
whole transfer to completion inside the register write that started it, so
the interrupt is there before the first poll looks for it.
